<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1903 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Dessalinienne</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Haiti's national anthem, a march written in 1903 for the 100th anniversary of the Haitian Revolution.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Justin Lhérisson</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Nicolas Geffrard</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Haitians</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Haiti</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1903; national anthem from 1904</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Justin Lhérisson (1903))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-dessalinienne">La Dessalinienne</a>&quot; is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, a march with words by Justin Lhérisson and music by Nicolas Geffrard. It is named in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a>, the revolutionary leader and first ruler of Haiti; the historian Clément Lanier suggested the title.</p><p>In 1903, to mark the 100th anniversary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, a competition was held for a national anthem. The judges preferred Lhérisson's verse and Geffrard's martial music to &quot;L'Artibonitienne&quot; by the diplomat Louis Edouard Pouget. The anthem was first performed in October 1903, at a celebration of the Armée Indigène's entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> organised by the Association du Petit Théâtre, sung by Auguste de Pradines, known as Kandjo. The words and music were printed at Bernard's in Port-au-Prince and sent throughout the country during that week. It was officially adopted as the national anthem in 1904.</p><p>Because one verse is short, performances are often lengthened by singing the first verse followed at once by the last. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-creole">Haitian Creole</a> version, &quot;Desalinyèn&quot;, was made by Raymond A. Moyse, and the singer Ansy Dérose (1934–1998) helped make it popular in 1980.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic march in honour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> and of Dessalines, written for its centenary. Its tone is martial and solemn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Pays, pour les Ancêtres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Country, for the Ancestors</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Marchons unis, marchons unis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us march united, let us march united</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Dans nos rangs point de traîtres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In our ranks no traitors</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Du sol soyons seuls maîtres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the soil let us be sole masters</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Marchons unis, marchons unis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us march united, let us march united</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Pays, pour les Ancêtres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Country, for the Ancestors</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Marchons, marchons, marchons unis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us march, march, march united</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Pays, pour les Ancêtres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Country, for the Ancestors</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour les Aïeux, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Forefathers, for the Fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Bêchons joyeux, bêchons joyeux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us dig joyfully, let us dig joyfully</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand le champ fructifie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the field bears fruit</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L'âme se fortifie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The soul grows strong</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Bêchons joyeux, bêchons joyeux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us dig joyfully, let us dig joyfully</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour les Aïeux, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Forefathers, for the Fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Bêchons, bêchons, bêchons joyeux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us dig, dig, dig joyfully</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour les Aïeux, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Forefathers, for the Fatherland</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Pays et pour nos Pères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Country and for our Fathers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Formons des Fils, formons des Fils</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us raise Sons, let us raise Sons</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Libres, forts et prospères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Free, strong and prosperous</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Toujours nous serons frères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall always be brothers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Formons des Fils, formons des Fils</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us raise Sons, let us raise Sons</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Pays et pour nos Pères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Country and for our Fathers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Formons, formons, formons des Fils</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us raise, raise, raise Sons</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Pays et pour nos Pères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Country and for our Fathers</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour les Aïeux, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Forefathers, for the Fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">O Dieu des Preux, O Dieu des Preux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O God of the Valiant, O God of the Valiant</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sous ta garde infinie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under your infinite protection</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Prends nos droits, notre vie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Take our rights, our life</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">O Dieu des Preux, O Dieu des Preux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O God of the Valiant, O God of the Valiant</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour les Aïeux, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Forefathers, for the Fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">O Dieu, O Dieu, O Dieu des Preux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O God, O God, O God of the Valiant</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour les Aïeux, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Forefathers, for the Fatherland</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Drapeau, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Flag, for the Fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mourir est beau, mourir est beau</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To die is beautiful, to die is beautiful</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Notre passé nous crie:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our past cries out to us:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ayez l'âme aguerrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have hardened souls</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mourir est beau, mourir est beau</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To die is beautiful, to die is beautiful</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Drapeau, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Flag, for the Fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mourir, mourir, mourir est beau</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To die, to die, to die is beautiful</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le Drapeau, pour la Patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the Flag, for the Fatherland</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Justin Lhérisson (1903); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (French Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Dessalinienne" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>'Ancêtres' is rendered Ancestors and 'Aïeux' Forefathers, as the original uses two words.</li><li>line 3.2: 'Formons des Fils', literally 'let us form sons': raise and train them.</li><li>line 4.2: 'Preux', the valiant, the brave (an old word for knights).</li><li>line 5.4: 'aguerrie', hardened to war, battle-tried.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Desalinyèn (Raymond A. Moyse)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Haitian Creole version, popularised by Ansy Dérose in 1980</span></li></ul></section>
